### Q22: Explain the volatile keyword and when to use it
```c
// WITHOUT volatile - WRONG for hardware/concurrent access
int flag = 0;
while (!flag) {  // May optimize this to: while (true)!
    // Wait for flag to change
}
interrupt_handler() {
    flag = 1;  // Thread/ISR changes flag
}

// WITH volatile - CORRECT
volatile int flag = 0;
while (!flag) {  // Compiler MUST check flag each iteration
    // Wait for flag to change
}
interrupt_handler() {
    flag = 1;
}

// Use cases:
// 1. Hardware registers
volatile int *timer = (int *)0x40000000;
int elapsed = *timer;  // Read from hardware each time

// 2. Signal handlers
volatile sig_atomic_t received_signal = 0;
void signal_handler(int sig) {
    received_signal = 1;
}

// 3. Thread communication (though atomics are better)
volatile int shared = 0;
```

**Important:**
- Prevents compiler optimizations
- Forces memory read/write
- Necessary for hardware/concurrent code
- NOT a substitute for synchronization

